page ref_host {
  title = "Host configuration file"
  chapter_header "Host configuration file" {
    kicker       = "Reference · reference"
    reading_time = "5 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-21"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    The host configuration file holds the settings that belong to this
    machine rather than to any lab: the subnet pool, the DNS suffix, the
    pre-shared key for cross-host segments, the viewer command, the
    shared-app clients, the ffmpeg console recordings are encoded with, the
    workspace syncer's size guard, and the host's GPUs.
    [Host configuration and WSL 2](guide_host) explains when to change each one; this chapter lists the fields.
  DOC

  h2 "Where it lives"
  p <<DOC
    The file is `config.wcl` in vmlab's XDG config directory, which is
    `~/.config/vmlab/` unless `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` moves it. See
    [Files and directories](ref_paths). It is optional: when the file is
    absent every default applies. When present it must start with
    `import <vmlab-host.wcl>`, and a file without the import is rejected
    with an error naming the missing line. The daemons read it when they
    start, so a change takes effect on the next `vmlab up` after the
    supervisor restarts.
  DOC
  code wcl { filename = "~/.config/vmlab/config.wcl" source = <<'SRC'
import <vmlab-host.wcl>

host {
  subnet_pool = "10.99.0.0/16"
  dns_suffix  = "lab.local"
  psk         = "shared-secret"
  viewer      = "vncviewer {}"
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "host {}"
  p <<DOC
    The block of host-wide settings; the file carries at most one, beside
    any host GPUs (below). Every field is an override: an absent
    field leaves the default in place, and a malformed one is reported with
    its line rather than silently ignored.
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
host {
  subnet_pool          = "10.213.0.0/16"
  dns_suffix           = "vmlab.internal"
  dns_upstream         = "1.1.1.1:53"
  disk_low_percent     = 10
  psk                  = "…"
  trunk_port           = 13947
  viewer               = "vncviewer {}"
  waypipe              = "/opt/waypipe/bin/waypipe"
  freerdp              = "/usr/bin/xfreerdp3"
  ffmpeg               = "/opt/ffmpeg/bin/ffmpeg"
  fastpath             = "auto"
  oci_chunk_size       = 512MiB
  config_weave_bin_dir = "~/.local/share/config-weave/bin"
  workspace_max_file   = 256MiB
  firmware_dir         = "/opt/vmlab-firmware"
  vmlab_hv             = "/opt/vmlab-hv/bin/vmlab-hv"
  hypervisor {
    container = "qemu"
    vm        = "qemu"
  }
}
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`subnet_pool`" | "utf8" | "`10.213.0.0/16`" | "CIDR the automatic /24 segment subnets are carved from." |
      | "`dns_suffix`" | "utf8" | "`vmlab.internal`" | "Suffix for auto-registered machine names, `<vm>.<lab>.<suffix>`." |
      | "`dns_upstream`" | "utf8" | "host resolver" | "Upstream resolver as `ip[:port]` for queries the lab DNS cannot answer." |
      | "`disk_low_percent`" | "i64" | "`10`" | "Free-space percentage, 0 to 100, below which the `host.disk_low` watchdog fires." |
      | "`psk`" | "utf8" | "none" | "Pre-shared key for cross-host segment links. Set the same value on both hosts." |
      | "`trunk_port`" | "i64" | "`13947`" | "TCP port the supervisor listens on for inbound cross-host segment trunks, 1 to 65535." |
      | "`viewer`" | "utf8" | "none" | "VNC viewer command; `{}` is replaced by the target." |
      | "`waypipe`" | "utf8" | "`waypipe` on `PATH`" | "Path to the host's waypipe client for [shared apps](guide_apps) from Linux guests." |
      | "`freerdp`" | "utf8" | "`xfreerdp3`, then `xfreerdp`, on `PATH`" | "Path to the FreeRDP 3 client for [shared apps](guide_apps) from Windows guests." |
      | "`ffmpeg`" | "utf8" | "`ffmpeg` on `PATH`" | "Path to the ffmpeg that encodes [console recordings](guide_recording). It needs `libx264` for `.mp4` and `libvpx-vp9` for `.webm`." |
      | "`fastpath`" | "utf8" | "`auto`" | "Network fast path: `auto` probes, `off` forces userspace, `sockmap` and `afxdp` force a kernel tier." |
      | "`oci_chunk_size`" | "ByteSize" | "`512MiB`" | "OCI layer chunk size for `vmlab template push`." |
      | "`config_weave_bin_dir`" | "utf8" | "`~/.local/share/config-weave/bin`" | "Directory holding the config-weave guest binaries playbooks push." |
      | "`workspace_max_file`" | "ByteSize" | "`256MiB`" | "Workspace syncer per-file size guard. A larger file is refused by name." |
      | "`firmware_dir`" | "utf8" | "none" | "UEFI firmware override, laid out `<dir>/<arch>/` with the bundled set's file names. Searched before vmlab's bundled firmware and the host's." |
      | "`vmlab_hv`" | "utf8" | "`vmlab-hv` on PATH" | "The vmlab-hv binary that machines on `hypervisor = \"vmlab\"` run. `up` checks that it exists before it starts anything." |
      | "`hypervisor {}`" | "child" | "`qemu` for both" | "The hypervisor a machine runs on when it does not name one, by machine kind: `container` and `vm`, each `qemu` or `vmlab`. Keyed by kind because vmlab-hv takes machines over one phase at a time. A machine's own `hypervisor` wins." |
  }
  p "The parser enforces these rules, and reports every violation in one pass:"
  list {
    li "`subnet_pool` is a well-formed CIDR."
    li "`disk_low_percent` is between 0 and 100."
    li "`trunk_port` is between 1 and 65535."
    li "`fastpath` is one of `auto`, `off`, `sockmap`, `afxdp`. The `VMLAB_FASTPATH` environment variable selects the same values."
    li "`oci_chunk_size` and `workspace_max_file` are non-negative sizes."
    li "`hypervisor { container, vm }` are each `qemu` or `vmlab`."
    li "A field the schema does not name is rejected with its position."
    li "`gpu_address`, removed after v0.24, is rejected with a message naming the `gpu` block that replaced it."
  }
  p <<DOC
    `workspace_max_file` is host config
    rather than a `@dev` argument because the cap is about this developer's
    link to the guest, not the lab everyone shares; the refusal message names
    the field. See [Dev machines and the workspace syncer](guide_dev).
    `firmware_dir` is authoritative for an architecture it has a directory
    for: a pair missing from `<dir>/<arch>/` is an error, never a fall back to
    firmware you did not choose. An architecture with no directory there uses
    the bundled set. See [Host configuration and WSL 2](guide_host).
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    `waypipe` and `freerdp` are read by `vmlab app run` itself rather than by
    a daemon, so a change applies to the next shared app with no restart.
    Neither path is checked when the file is read. A wrong one fails when
    `vmlab app run` starts it, naming the path. See [Shared apps](guide_apps).
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    `ffmpeg` is read by the lab daemon when a recording starts, and checked
    then: the daemon runs it with `-encoders` and refuses a recording whose
    format it cannot write, naming this key. Nothing looks for ffmpeg at
    `vmlab up` unless `--record` is given. See
    [Console recording](guide_recording).
  DOC
  h2 "gpu \"<name>\" {}"
  p <<DOC
    A host GPU: a GPU on this machine, named so a lab's `gpu {}` can ask for
    it with `host_gpu` rather than carry a PCI address. Declare zero or more,
    beside `host {}`. See [gpu {}](ref_vm) for how a lab picks one.
  DOC
  code wcl { source = <<'SRC'
gpu "AMD1"    { address = "0000:72:00.0" }
gpu "Nvidia1" {
  address     = "0000:01:00.0"
  default     = true
  qemu_binary = "/opt/qemu-vmlab/bin/qemu-system-x86_64"
  qemu_env    = { VKR_DEBUG: "validate" }
}
gpu "Nvidia2" { address = "0000:05:00.0"  vulkan_icd = "/usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/nvidia_icd.json" }
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "label" | "utf8" | "required" | "The name. A lab's `host_gpu` matches it case-insensitively." |
      | "`address`" | "utf8" | "required" | "Host PCI address, as under `/sys/bus/pci/devices/`." |
      | "`vulkan_icd`" | "utf8" | "none" | "Path to a Vulkan ICD JSON a `vulkan` guest's renderer loads, set as `VK_DRIVER_FILES`. Overrides the driver picked from the GPU's kernel driver." |
      | "`default`" | "bool" | "`false`" | "The GPU a lab gets when it names none, or names one this host lacks under virgl or vulkan. A lone `gpu` block is the default without it." |
      | "`qemu_binary`" | "utf8" | "none" | "Absolute path to the QEMU every VM on this GPU runs, in every mode including passthrough, instead of `qemu-system-<arch>` from `PATH`. vmlab also passes it `-L <dir>` (see below)." |
      | "`qemu_env`" | "record of utf8" | "none" | "Variables set in that VM's QEMU process, written `{ KEY: \"value\" }`. Applied last, so a key here beats the Vulkan loader variable vmlab derives." |
      | "`keepawake`" | "utf8" | "`auto`" | "Whether the supervisor holds this GPU out of runtime suspend from the first virgl or vulkan VM started on it until the supervisor stops. `auto`: only an NVIDIA GPU that can runtime-suspend. `on`: always, on any driver with EGL. `off`: never. See below." |
  }
  p "The parser enforces these rules:"
  list {
    li "Every block has a label and an `address`."
    li "Two labels that differ only in case are one name declared twice, which is an error."
    li "At most one block carries `default = true`."
    li "`qemu_binary` is an absolute path."
    li "Every `qemu_env` value is a string. Each key is a WCL identifier, so it is never empty and never contains `=`."
    li "`keepawake` is `auto`, `on` or `off`."
  }
  p <<DOC
    The rest is checked when a lab uses the GPU, by `vmlab validate` and at
    VM start, naming the GPU: its address must name a PCI device, a virgl or
    vulkan guest needs that device to have a DRM render node, and its
    `vulkan_icd` must exist. A GPU bound to `vfio-pci` for passthrough has no
    render node and needs none. Its `qemu_binary` must be an executable
    file. A file named `qemu-system-<arch>` must also match the VM's QEMU
    arch, or the check refuses it and names both arches. A file with any
    other name is used as given.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    A VM whose host GPU sets `qemu_binary` gets `-L <dir>`, where `<dir>` is
    the stock QEMU's data directory: `<prefix>/share/qemu` for the
    `qemu-system-<arch>` on `PATH`, if that directory exists. A minimal build
    therefore finds the firmware and option ROMs it was not installed with.
    QEMU searches its own data directory after the `-L` paths, so a full
    build loses nothing. With no stock QEMU on `PATH`, vmlab adds no `-L`.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    vmlab first removes the inherited `VK_*` loader variables and the
    PCI-filter layer's variables from the QEMU process's environment, then
    sets the ones it derives: the driver filter and, under `vulkan`,
    `VK_ADD_IMPLICIT_LAYER_PATH` and `VMLAB_VK_PCI_ADDRESS` for vmlab's
    `VK_LAYER_VMLAB_pci_filter`, which leaves the guest only the device at
    this GPU's `address`. `qemu_env` is applied after that, and an explicit
    host setting beats a derived one: a `VK_LOADER_DRIVERS_SELECT` in
    `qemu_env` replaces vmlab's, and `VMLAB_VK_PCI_FILTER_DISABLE: "1"`
    turns the layer off. The
    `vmlab: env:` line in `qemu.log` lists every variable vmlab set,
    `qemu_env` included, with its value. `vmlab status -v` shows
    `qemu=<path>` after `host_gpu=` for a VM that ran a `qemu_binary`, then
    that same set as `env=NAME=value,...` by name, values in full;
    `vmlab status --json` carries it as the VM's `qemu_env` object. Both
    show what the VM was started with, not the host config as it is now.
    Template builds never have a `gpu {}`, so they always run the stock QEMU.
  DOC
  h3 "Runtime power management"
  p <<DOC
    A GPU with runtime power management on (`power/control` is `auto`) is
    powered down by its driver once nothing has used it for a few seconds.
    Between one lab's `vmlab down` and the next `vmlab up` nothing uses it.
    On an NVIDIA GPU the resume after that boots the GSP firmware again,
    and a resume that fails (`Xid 119`) loses the GPU until the host
    reboots. vmlab handles this three ways:
  DOC
  list {
    li "A GPU whose `power/runtime_status` is `error` is refused by `vmlab validate` and at VM start, naming the GPU and its address. Its driver has already lost it, and QEMU would abort on it some seconds after the VM was reported up. Reboot the host."
    li "`vmlab up` warns once about an NVIDIA GPU a virgl or vulkan VM renders on that can runtime-suspend: `power/control` is `auto` and the driver does not report runtime D3 disabled in `/proc/driver/nvidia/gpus/<address>/power`. NVIDIA leaves `power/control` at `auto` on desktop cards whose runtime D3 it has turned off, so those get no warning."
    li "Under `keepawake` (default `auto`, the same NVIDIA condition) the supervisor starts a small helper, `vmlab-gpu-keepawake`, before the first such VM on the GPU starts. The helper opens an OpenGL ES context on the GPU through EGL and clears a 16x16 renderbuffer every 100 ms. A context alone does not stop the NVIDIA driver from suspending the GPU, but small regular work does. The helper needs no root and about 6 MiB of video memory, and runs until `vmlab daemon stop`. It exits by itself if the supervisor dies. `vmlab status -v` shows `keepawake=` after `host_gpu=`."
  }
  p <<DOC
    The helper covers `down` then `up` while the supervisor runs. It does
    not cover a supervisor restart, and the GPU is held only after a VM has
    started on it. To keep an NVIDIA GPU powered at all times, fix it on the
    host as root, with one of these:
  DOC
  code text { source = <<'SRC'
# /etc/udev/rules.d/99-nvidia-no-runtime-pm.rules: keep every NVIDIA GPU powered, every boot
ACTION=="add|bind", SUBSYSTEM=="pci", ATTR{vendor}=="0x10de", ATTR{class}=="0x030000", ATTR{power/control}="on"

# or turn runtime D3 off in the driver (/etc/modprobe.d/nvidia-no-rtd3.conf, then reboot;
# regenerate the initramfs if it loads nvidia)
options nvidia NVreg_DynamicPowerManagement=0x00

# or, until the next reboot, for one GPU
echo on | sudo tee /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/power/control
SRC
  }
  p <<DOC
    All three keep the GPU powered while idle, which costs battery on a
    laptop. The helper holds it only while the supervisor runs.
  DOC

  callout "A neutral fast path" { kind = :info body = <<DOC
    `auto` never selects `sockmap`; it was measured slower than the userspace
    fabric and exists for explicit evaluation. Both kernel tiers need CAP_BPF
    and CAP_NET_ADMIN, and a daemon that cannot prove a tier works on its host
    falls back to userspace silently. `vmlab fastpath` reports the tier in
    use.
  DOC
  }
}
